<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Joseph Kasavubu</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1915–1969</span> · Also known as Joseph Kasa-Vubu</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Joseph Kasavubu was the first president of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/republic-of-the-congo">Republic of the Congo</a> (now the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/democratic-republic-of-the-congo">Democratic Republic of the Congo</a>), from independence in 1960 to 1965. As leader of ABAKO, a party of the Bakongo, he campaigned for independence. In office he clashed with prime minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patrice-lumumba">Patrice Lumumba</a>, and he was deposed in Joseph-Désiré Mobutu's coup of November 1965.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/joseph-kasavubu.jpg" alt="Joseph Kasavubu" width="320" height="469" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: unknown photographer / Anefo · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Joseph_Kasa-Vubu_at_the_Belgo-Congolese_Round_Table_Conference.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
